{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's claim that Popper was a 'crypto-inductivist' relying on 'metaphysical realism' to save his system is historically inaccurate and contradicts Popper's explicit rejection of such commitments. Popper defended the preference for better-corroborated theories not via hidden induction or realist metaphysics, but through the non-inductive logic of verisimilitude (truth-likeness) and the methodological rule that preferring severely tested theories maximizes the chance of eliminating error. The target fails to engage with Popper's actual solution (verisimilitude) and instead attacks a strawman version of Popper that relies on the very metaphysical assumptions he explicitly argued against.","premise":"Popper explicitly rejected metaphysical realism as a basis for scientific preference and proposed verisimilitude as the non-inductive justification for preferring corroborated theories.","premises_essential":["e05373652830fcd4277b54cd9b7cb5efee2f23269834058fcc2bbd3db6c20d73"],"successor_question":"How does the target's assertion of 'crypto-inductivism' reconcile with Popper's detailed arguments in 'Conjectures and Refutations' regarding verisimilitude as a non-probabilistic, non-inductive measure of theoretical progress?","target":"e05373652830fcd4277b54cd9b7cb5efee2f23269834058fcc2bbd3db6c20d73"}]}